'''L'empresa del Canal i el Port''' ({{lang|ca|L'empresa del Canal i el Port}}), commonly known as '''ECP''', is a [[Balboa|Balboan]] public-private partnership that provides port services and canal transit services for the [[Balboa Canal]]. It is one of the largest companies listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] and a central institution in the Balboan economy. The Government of Balboa holds a 50 per cent stake; the remainder is publicly traded.

ECP operates the port infrastructure and provides vessel handling, pilotage, towage, berthing, cargo handling, and maintenance services at both the [[Portnou|Shendan terminus]] and the [[Sant Llàtzer|Aethiopian terminus]] of the canal. The former commercial port at Sant Cristòfor was relocated to Portnou in the late twentieth century; ECP now operates only the cruise terminal at the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]]. Canal transit fees, however, accrue directly to the government and do not pass through the company. Defence and security of the canal and port facilities remain under the authority of the [[Ministry of Interior (Balboa)|Ministry of Interior]]. ECP pays rent to the government for its operating concession.

== History ==

=== Ministry of Interior era ===

From the return of the canal to Balboan sovereignty under the [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973), all canal and port operations were administered directly by the [[Ministry of Interior (Balboa)|Ministry of Interior]]. The ministry operated the ports, collected transit fees, maintained the lock systems, and managed canal security through its own personnel. This arrangement was a natural consequence of the canal's strategic importance — the waterway was the country's most valuable asset, and the government was unwilling to entrust its operation to any entity outside direct state control so soon after reunification.

By the early 1980s, however, the limitations of ministerial administration had become apparent. The ministry's bureaucratic structure was poorly suited to the commercial demands of a growing international port, and the canal's infrastructure was deteriorating for lack of investment. Traffic volumes were increasing, and the port facilities at Portnou and Sant Llàtzer required modernisation that the ministry could not finance within the constraints of the annual budget.

=== Spin-off and public-private partnership (1986) ===

In 1986, the [[Balboan People's Party|BPP]] government spun off the port and canal service operations into a newly created public-private partnership, L'empresa del Canal i el Port. The government retained a 50 per cent stake and issued the remaining 50 per cent as publicly traded shares on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]]. The spin-off was designed to give the company access to private capital for infrastructure investment while preserving government control over the canal's strategic functions.

The founding legislation established a clear division of responsibilities:

* '''Canal transit fees''' continue to accrue directly to the Government of Balboa and are not collected by ECP.
* '''Defence and security''' of the canal and port facilities remain under the authority of the [[Ministry of Interior (Balboa)|Ministry of Interior]].
* '''Port services and canal operations''' — including pilotage, towage, berthing, mooring, cargo handling, vessel servicing, and infrastructure maintenance — are operated by ECP.
* '''Rent''': ECP pays an annual concession rent to the government for the right to operate the port and canal service facilities.

The arrangement has been broadly stable since 1986, though it has been periodically renegotiated to adjust the rent, expand the company's service mandate, and address capital investment needs.

== Operations ==

ECP's operations are concentrated at three main facilities:

* [[Portnou]] — the canal's Shendan terminus and the largest port facility in Balboa. Portnou handles container transshipment, bulk cargo, and petroleum products, and is the primary western gateway for canal traffic.
* [[Sant Llàtzer]] — the canal's Aethiopian terminus, handling Aethiopian Ocean traffic and the eastern approach to the canal.
* [[Sant Cristòfor Cruise Terminal]] — the sole remaining port facility on the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]], handling cruise ship arrivals and departures. The former commercial port was relocated to Portnou in the late twentieth century, and the former docklands have been redeveloped for residential and commercial use.

The company provides a full range of port services at each facility, including pilotage, towage, berthing, mooring, cargo handling, vessel servicing, and infrastructure maintenance. It is also responsible for the day-to-day maintenance of the canal's lock systems and approach channels, though major capital works remain subject to government approval and financing.

== Relationship with the Balboa Canal Authority ==

{{main|Balboa Canal Authority}}

The [[Balboa Canal Authority]] is the state agency that holds sovereign responsibility for the canal under the [[Treaty of Areza]]. The Authority sets transit policy, determines toll rates, and ensures compliance with the treaty's non-discrimination and access provisions. ECP operates the canal's service infrastructure under concession to the Authority, but does not set toll policy or collect transit fees. The two entities work in close operational coordination but maintain separate governance and financial structures.

